# Environment for the agent runtime.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` for your own machine, and keep that copy out of git:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Nothing in this file is a credential. The design keeps every provider credential inside the broker
# container, which is the only place a key or a token lives. The agent container holds a dummy token and
# reaches the broker over an internal network, so a leaked container leaks nothing usable.
#
# The launchers read these from the environment. Nothing here needs to be set to run the quality gate or
# the test suites; the names matter when you stand up the sandbox and the console.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The broker — the only component that holds credentials
#    Read by sandbox/broker/broker.py. The launcher passes the first three in.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# How the broker presents your Claude credential: `oauth` uses the credentials file, `api_key` uses the
# key below. Leave it on oauth unless you know why you are changing it.
ANTHROPIC_MODE=oauth

# Path to the Claude credentials file, INSIDE the broker container. The launcher mounts your own
# ~/.claude directory at /secrets/claude, so this path resolves to your real credentials.
CLAUDE_CRED=/secrets/claude/.credentials.json

# Used only when ANTHROPIC_MODE=api_key. Leave it empty for oauth.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# File holding a DeepSeek or OpenAI-compatible key, INSIDE the broker container. The launcher mounts the
# state directory at /state, so this is <state dir>/deepseek.key on your host.
OPENAI_KEY_FILE=/state/deepseek.key

# The upstream the broker forwards OpenAI-compatible calls to.
OPENAI_UPSTREAM=https://api.deepseek.com

# The port the broker listens on inside its network.
PORT=4000

# Where the broker writes a copy of the credentials file before it rewrites it in place. A backup is
# still a credential: the broker creates these files with mode 0600.
BACKUP_DIR=/state/backups

# The public OAuth client identifier of the Claude CLI. It ships inside the CLI binary and is not a
# secret. Set it only to pin a different client.
# CLAUDE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. The agent container — set by the launcher, shown so you can read them
#    You do not need to export these. sandbox/run-agent.sh sets them at container creation.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the container's Claude CLI sends its API calls: the broker, resolved on the internal network.
ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=http://console-broker:4000

# The placeholder the container sends as its authorization token. It is deliberately not a credential;
# the broker replaces it with the real one upstream. Never put a real token here.
ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=sandbox-dummy-token

# Keeps the CLI from calling home for optional telemetry, which an isolated container cannot reach.
CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_NONESSENTIAL_TRAFFIC=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. The launcher knobs — read by sandbox/run-agent.sh and scripts/run-agent.sh
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The checkout to mount at /workspace. Defaults to ~/agentic-console.
# REPO=$HOME/agentic-console

# The image the agent container runs.
IMAGE=agent-sandbox:console-m1

# The image the broker container runs. The launcher builds it from sandbox/broker if it is missing.
BROKER_IMAGE=console-sandbox-broker:local

# The broker container's name, and the host name the agent container resolves.
BROKER_NAME=console-broker

# The two networks. The agent container joins the internal one; the broker joins both so it can reach
# the upstream provider.
NET=console-net
# The MCP servers listen on the internal network. The launcher creates it if it is missing:
#     docker network create --internal console-internal

# The cargo target-dir cache, so rebuilds are incremental. One per workspace: two containers sharing a
# target dir serialise on cargo's build lock instead of running in parallel. Defaults to <slug>-cargo-target.
# TARGET_VOL=agentic-console-cargo-target
#
# The cargo REGISTRY volume is mounted by the engine itself, so a container can resolve crates with no
# network access. It is created from the reference setup and kept, and it is not a knob:
#     docker volume inspect console-cargo-registry

# The container name. Defaults to agent-<slug of the workspace directory>, so two checkouts never collide.
# AGENT_NAME=agent-agentic-console

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Host-side state — created by sandbox/stage-secrets.sh, outside the repository
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The state directory. It holds the staged key material and the broker's backups, mode 0600, and the
# launcher mounts it at /state. It defaults to ~/.config/console-sandbox.
# STATE=$HOME/.config/console-sandbox

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. What must never appear in this file
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Real keys, tokens, passwords or credential files. Stage them instead, from your own machine, outside
# the repository:
#
#     sandbox/stage-secrets.sh
#
# It writes the broker's key material into the state directory with mode 0600 and copies nothing into
# the repository.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A note on the Module 3 record
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# sandbox/README-m3.md and sandbox/run-agent-m3.sh keep the names they were measured under, because they
# record the Module 3 sandbox exercise: the checkpoint answers quote measurements taken on those names.
# The runtime this repository actually runs is sandbox/run-agent.sh, and the names above are its names.
